WHY TWO LICENSES
================

The server is AGPL-3.0. OpenBooks is operated as a hosted service from the
same image it ships for self-hosting, and the AGPL's network-use provision
(section 13) is what keeps a hosted fork from taking the work private. This
is a deliberate business choice, not an accident of copy-paste.

The contract packages are Apache-2.0. @openbooks/plugin-api and the published
OpenAPI description exist so that other software can be written against
OpenBooks. A copyleft license on an interface definition would defeat that
purpose, so the interfaces are permissive and carry an explicit linking
exception clarifying that depending on them does not, by itself, pull a
module under the AGPL.

The exception is narrow on purpose. It does not grant permission to convey a
combined work of the AGPL server plus a bundled third-party module on
non-AGPL terms. Integrations are meant to be built as external clients of the
public HTTP API and OAuth authorization server, where no combination with
AGPL-licensed code occurs at all. See section 3(b) and section 4 of
./packages/plugin-api/LICENSE.


